Draining the inbox meant asking one claude session to loop the skill,
so the session's own judgment decided when to stop. All the passes also
shared one context, which grew with every thread.

just process-email runs the loop from outside the model. It picks each
message with get-oldest-inbox-message and gives its id to a fresh
claude -p. It ends when the inbox is empty. claude's output goes to
/dev/null, so control never depends on what the model prints. Whether a
pass worked is read from the notmuch index: if its message is still in
the inbox, the loop stops with an error instead of retrying that
message forever.
---
change-id: xvnlmpwlrqonwvmusyprlolnwuyqqylx

 Justfile | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Justfile b/Justfile
index 4c6412e..02d15f5 100644
--- a/Justfile
+++ b/Justfile
@@ -71,3 +71,25 @@ claude-mail-purge:
 
 reauthor *revset:
   jj metaedit --update-author {{revset}}
+
+# print the oldest message in the lab-devel inbox, if any
+@get-oldest-inbox-message:
+  notmuch search --sort=oldest-first --output=messages --limit=1 tag:inbox
+
+# answer the lab-devel inbox, one claude pass per message, oldest first
+process-email:
+  #!/bin/sh
+  set -eu
+  while :; do
+    notmuch new --quiet
+    id="$(just get-oldest-inbox-message)"
+    [ -n "${id}" ] || break
+    echo "process-email: ${id}"
+    claude --permission-mode auto -p "/process-email ${id}" \
+      </dev/null >/dev/null
+    notmuch new --quiet
+    if [ "$(notmuch count "${id} and tag:inbox")" -ne 0 ]; then
+      echo "E: process-email: ${id} is still in the inbox" >&2
+      exit 1
+    fi
+  done
